Satellite Region Classifier for Natural Land Cover

Problem statement: We want to build a satellite-image classifier based on our biome/region idea. To keep the scope manageable, we will classify four natural land-cover types: forest, herbaceous vegetation, river, and sea/lake. These are visible surface categories, rather than complete ecological biomes. The input will be one overhead RGB image patch, and the output will be one class label. This could help organize satellite imagery for environmental mapping. Our targets are at least 90% test accuracy and 0.85 macro-F1, with an improvement over a simpler baseline; these are goals, not results.

Data: We will use 11,500 images from four classes in EuroSAT, whose full dataset contains 27,000 labeled 64-by-64 patches across ten classes [1]. The RGB JPEGs have image-level class labels, so we will not need bounding-box annotations. The source is Sentinel-2 imagery from Europe, with different landscapes and acquisition times. It mainly covers low-cloud overhead views; heavy cloud cover, snow/ice, other sensors, and worldwide biome diversity are limitations. We will check duplicates before splitting. RGB also leaves out the infrared bands that could help distinguish vegetation.

Approach: Our baseline will use color histograms and image-gradient statistics with a k-nearest-neighbor classifier. This connects to histograms and finite-difference filters from class, but similar colors can make different vegetation types hard to separate. We will compare it with an ImageNet-pretrained ResNet-18 CNN [2], replacing its final layer with four outputs. We will train that layer first, then fine-tune the last convolutional block. This should require less training than starting from scratch. We will resize inputs consistently and apply the pretrained model's normalization. We will also compare unmodified images with gamma adjustment and mild Gaussian filtering to see whether our course's point operations and smoothing help or remove useful texture.

Evaluation plan: We will reserve 15% of each class for final testing and use three-fold cross-validation on the remaining data to select preprocessing and parameters. Duplicates will not cross splits, and nearby patches will stay together where location metadata permits. We will freeze all choices before testing. Accuracy will show overall performance; macro-F1 and per-class recall will expose uneven performance. A confusion matrix will show which classes get mixed up. We will compare both models on the same splits and report inference time. Stress tests will separately apply 0.7x/1.3x brightness changes, clipping to the valid range, and Gaussian blur with sigma values of 1 and 2 pixels at the original resolution. We will inspect vegetation-water boundaries for failures. These tests will not establish worldwide generalization.

Prior work: So far, we have brainstormed the topic and discussed class definitions and data normalization. Our background includes CNN training/testing and the image-processing exercises from class. We have not trained or evaluated a model for this project yet.

References: [1] P. Helber et al., EuroSAT: A Novel Dataset and Deep Learning Benchmark for Land Use and Land Cover Classification, 2019. https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.00029; dataset: https://github.com/phelber/EuroSAT; class counts checked from filename lists linked in TorchGeo: https://github.com/torchgeo/torchgeo/blob/main/torchgeo/datasets/eurosat.py

[2] PyTorch, Torchvision ResNet-18 documentation. https://docs.pytorch.org/vision/stable/models/generated/torchvision.models.resnet18.html